1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,234
A script is only as safe as what it handles.

2
00:00:03,234 --> 00:00:04,539
Passwords and tokens.

3
00:00:04,539 --> 00:00:06,092
Who is allowed to run it.

4
00:00:06,092 --> 00:00:07,397
What a user types in.

5
00:00:07,397 --> 00:00:09,820
And what the shell does with that text.

6
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:11,187
Get one of them wrong,

7
00:00:11,187 --> 00:00:13,300
and a small script becomes a hole.

8
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:15,691
Today you learn four habits.

9
00:00:15,691 --> 00:00:17,729
Keep secrets out of the script.

10
00:00:17,729 --> 00:00:19,176
Lock down permissions.

11
00:00:19,176 --> 00:00:20,557
Validate every input.

12
00:00:20,557 --> 00:00:22,661
And never let input become code.

13
00:00:23,211 --> 00:00:25,900
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

14
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:27,802
Every secret you see is fake,

15
00:00:27,802 --> 00:00:29,376
made up for this lesson.

16
00:00:31,426 --> 00:00:31,875
First,

17
00:00:31,875 --> 00:00:32,473
secrets.

18
00:00:32,473 --> 00:00:35,389
Never write a password inside a script.

19
00:00:35,389 --> 00:00:37,034
Here is a bad example.

20
00:00:37,034 --> 00:00:38,679
Type: cat bad dot S H.

21
00:00:39,229 --> 00:00:41,297
The password sits in the file.

22
00:00:41,297 --> 00:00:43,434
Anyone who can read the script,

23
00:00:43,434 --> 00:00:45,501
or the repository it lands in,

24
00:00:45,501 --> 00:00:47,018
can read the password.

25
00:00:47,018 --> 00:00:49,361
The better way is a separate file.

26
00:00:49,361 --> 00:00:51,704
Check the permissions of this one.

27
00:00:51,704 --> 00:00:52,394
Type: L S,

28
00:00:52,394 --> 00:00:52,876
dash L,

29
00:00:52,876 --> 00:00:53,565
dot E N V.

30
00:00:54,115 --> 00:00:55,827
Read the first column.

31
00:00:55,827 --> 00:00:56,138
R W,

32
00:00:56,138 --> 00:00:56,682
then R,

33
00:00:56,682 --> 00:00:57,227
then R.

34
00:00:57,227 --> 00:00:58,782
The owner can write,

35
00:00:58,782 --> 00:01:01,116
but everyone else can read it.

36
00:01:01,116 --> 00:01:03,449
That is too open for a secret.

37
00:01:03,449 --> 00:01:03,993
Fix it.

38
00:01:03,993 --> 00:01:04,927
Type: chmod,

39
00:01:04,927 --> 00:01:05,860
six hundred,

40
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:06,638
dot E N V.

41
00:01:07,188 --> 00:01:07,491
Six,

42
00:01:07,491 --> 00:01:07,870
zero,

43
00:01:07,870 --> 00:01:10,900
zero means the owner can read and write,

44
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:13,172
and nobody else gets anything.

45
00:01:13,172 --> 00:01:14,081
Check again.

46
00:01:14,081 --> 00:01:14,839
Type: L S,

47
00:01:14,839 --> 00:01:15,369
dash L,

48
00:01:15,369 --> 00:01:16,127
dot E N V.

49
00:01:16,677 --> 00:01:18,820
Now only the owner has access.

50
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:19,177
Next,

51
00:01:19,177 --> 00:01:22,464
a script that loads the secret from that file,

52
00:01:22,464 --> 00:01:24,036
instead of holding it.

53
00:01:24,036 --> 00:01:25,679
Type: cat good dot S H.

54
00:01:26,229 --> 00:01:28,266
It reads the file with source,

55
00:01:28,266 --> 00:01:31,933
and prints only the last four characters of the token.

56
00:01:31,933 --> 00:01:34,514
Never print a whole secret into a log.

57
00:01:34,514 --> 00:01:34,989
Run it.

58
00:01:34,989 --> 00:01:36,619
Type: bash good dot S H.

59
00:01:37,169 --> 00:01:38,182
The script works,

60
00:01:38,182 --> 00:01:40,269
and the secret never appears in it.

61
00:01:40,269 --> 00:01:42,535
Keep that file out of version control,

62
00:01:42,535 --> 00:01:44,443
with an entry in dot git ignore.

63
00:01:46,493 --> 00:01:47,019
Second,

64
00:01:47,019 --> 00:01:49,501
permissions on the script itself.

65
00:01:49,501 --> 00:01:52,509
A new file is not executable by default.

66
00:01:52,509 --> 00:01:54,540
Look at this deploy script.

67
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:55,292
Type: L S,

68
00:01:55,292 --> 00:01:55,818
dash L,

69
00:01:55,818 --> 00:01:56,946
deploy dot S H.

70
00:01:57,496 --> 00:01:59,841
There is no X in the permissions,

71
00:01:59,841 --> 00:02:01,901
so it cannot be run directly.

72
00:02:01,901 --> 00:02:02,398
Try it.

73
00:02:02,398 --> 00:02:04,600
Type: dot slash deploy dot S H.

74
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:06,527
Permission denied.

75
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:09,663
Do not reach for chmod seven seven seven,

76
00:02:09,663 --> 00:02:12,264
which gives everyone full control.

77
00:02:12,264 --> 00:02:14,941
Give the owner only what is needed.

78
00:02:14,941 --> 00:02:15,859
Type: chmod,

79
00:02:15,859 --> 00:02:16,547
U plus X,

80
00:02:16,547 --> 00:02:17,694
deploy dot S H.

81
00:02:18,244 --> 00:02:24,538
That adds execute for the owner only. Check it. Type: L S, dash L, deploy dot S H.

82
00:02:25,088 --> 00:02:27,041
Now the owner has R W X,

83
00:02:27,041 --> 00:02:29,889
and nobody else has anything extra.

84
00:02:29,889 --> 00:02:30,458
Run it.

85
00:02:30,458 --> 00:02:32,981
Type: dot slash deploy dot S H.

86
00:02:33,531 --> 00:02:34,146
It runs.

87
00:02:34,146 --> 00:02:35,298
One more habit.

88
00:02:35,298 --> 00:02:39,294
The umask decides the permissions of every new file.

89
00:02:39,294 --> 00:02:41,368
Set it to zero seven seven,

90
00:02:41,368 --> 00:02:43,904
and files you create are private.

91
00:02:43,904 --> 00:02:46,132
Type: umask zero seven seven,

92
00:02:46,132 --> 00:02:46,901
semicolon,

93
00:02:46,901 --> 00:02:48,514
touch priv dot T X T,

94
00:02:48,514 --> 00:02:49,283
semicolon,

95
00:02:49,283 --> 00:02:49,590
L S,

96
00:02:49,590 --> 00:02:50,128
dash L,

97
00:02:50,128 --> 00:02:51,280
priv dot T X T.

98
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:52,992
R W for the owner,

99
00:02:52,992 --> 00:02:55,573
and nothing for the group or for others.

100
00:02:55,573 --> 00:02:59,574
Use a tight umask whenever a script writes secrets or reports.

101
00:03:01,624 --> 00:03:02,065
Third,

102
00:03:02,065 --> 00:03:03,315
input validation.

103
00:03:03,315 --> 00:03:05,814
Never trust what a user passes in.

104
00:03:05,814 --> 00:03:09,048
Here is a script that accepts a server name.

105
00:03:09,048 --> 00:03:11,032
Type: cat validate dot S H.

106
00:03:11,582 --> 00:03:13,975
It allows only lowercase letters,

107
00:03:13,975 --> 00:03:14,482
digits,

108
00:03:14,482 --> 00:03:15,933
dash and underscore,

109
00:03:15,933 --> 00:03:17,673
up to twenty characters.

110
00:03:17,673 --> 00:03:19,486
Anything else is refused,

111
00:03:19,486 --> 00:03:20,863
with exit code two.

112
00:03:20,863 --> 00:03:22,604
Run it with a good name.

113
00:03:22,604 --> 00:03:24,634
Type: bash validate dot S H,

114
00:03:24,634 --> 00:03:25,577
web zero one.

115
00:03:26,127 --> 00:03:26,831
Accepted.

116
00:03:26,831 --> 00:03:28,395
Now a hostile input,

117
00:03:28,395 --> 00:03:29,412
with a space,

118
00:03:29,412 --> 00:03:31,993
a semicolon and a delete command.

119
00:03:31,993 --> 00:03:34,184
Type: bash validate dot S H,

120
00:03:34,184 --> 00:03:36,765
and then in quotes: web zero one,

121
00:03:36,765 --> 00:03:37,547
semicolon,

122
00:03:37,547 --> 00:03:37,860
R M,

123
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:38,564
dash R F,

124
00:03:38,564 --> 00:03:39,033
slash.

125
00:03:39,583 --> 00:03:40,109
Refused.

126
00:03:40,109 --> 00:03:40,897
Nothing ran,

127
00:03:40,897 --> 00:03:43,589
because the input never passed the check.

128
00:03:43,589 --> 00:03:45,034
Look at the exit code.

129
00:03:45,034 --> 00:03:45,756
Type: echo,

130
00:03:45,756 --> 00:03:47,135
dollar question mark.

131
00:03:47,685 --> 00:03:47,970
Two.

132
00:03:47,970 --> 00:03:49,108
A clear refusal,

133
00:03:49,108 --> 00:03:52,595
and a clear signal for whoever called the script.

134
00:03:52,595 --> 00:03:53,733
One more attack,

135
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:56,366
a path that climbs out of the folder.

136
00:03:56,366 --> 00:03:58,358
Type: bash validate dot S H,

137
00:03:58,358 --> 00:03:59,781
dot dot slash E T C.

138
00:04:00,331 --> 00:04:01,328
Refused as well.

139
00:04:01,328 --> 00:04:02,699
Allow what you expect,

140
00:04:02,699 --> 00:04:04,382
and reject everything else.

141
00:04:04,382 --> 00:04:07,435
That is safer than trying to list everything bad.

142
00:04:09,485 --> 00:04:10,001
Fourth,

143
00:04:10,001 --> 00:04:10,739
injection.

144
00:04:10,739 --> 00:04:13,321
If a script lets input become code,

145
00:04:13,321 --> 00:04:15,608
the user controls your machine.

146
00:04:15,608 --> 00:04:17,304
Here is a risky script,

147
00:04:17,304 --> 00:04:18,116
using eval.

148
00:04:18,116 --> 00:04:19,960
Type: cat danger dot S H.

149
00:04:20,510 --> 00:04:22,766
Eval runs its text as a command.

150
00:04:22,766 --> 00:04:24,177
Pass it a file name,

151
00:04:24,177 --> 00:04:27,491
with a second command hidden after a semicolon.

152
00:04:27,491 --> 00:04:30,029
The hidden command is harmless here.

153
00:04:30,029 --> 00:04:31,581
It only prints a word.

154
00:04:31,581 --> 00:04:33,414
Type: bash danger dot S H,

155
00:04:33,414 --> 00:04:35,671
and then in quotes: app dot log,

156
00:04:35,671 --> 00:04:36,376
semicolon,

157
00:04:36,376 --> 00:04:37,363
echo INJECTED.

158
00:04:37,913 --> 00:04:39,810
The word INJECTED appeared.

159
00:04:39,810 --> 00:04:41,427
The hidden command ran,

160
00:04:41,427 --> 00:04:43,043
as if you had typed it.

161
00:04:43,043 --> 00:04:44,519
Now the safe version.

162
00:04:44,519 --> 00:04:46,276
Type: cat quoted dot S H.

163
00:04:46,826 --> 00:04:47,379
No eval,

164
00:04:47,379 --> 00:04:49,939
and the variable is in double quotes,

165
00:04:49,939 --> 00:04:51,738
with two dashes before it,

166
00:04:51,738 --> 00:04:53,813
so it can only be a file name.

167
00:04:53,813 --> 00:04:55,681
Run it with the same input.

168
00:04:55,681 --> 00:04:57,480
Type: bash quoted dot S H,

169
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:59,693
and then in quotes: app dot log,

170
00:04:59,693 --> 00:05:00,385
semicolon,

171
00:05:00,385 --> 00:05:01,354
echo INJECTED.

172
00:05:01,904 --> 00:05:03,307
Nothing was injected.

173
00:05:03,307 --> 00:05:06,249
The whole text was treated as one file name,

174
00:05:06,249 --> 00:05:07,652
which does not exist.

175
00:05:07,652 --> 00:05:09,056
Quote your variables,

176
00:05:09,056 --> 00:05:10,928
and never use eval on input.

177
00:05:12,978 --> 00:05:18,119
Now put the four habits together, in one script. Type: cat secure dot S H.

178
00:05:18,669 --> 00:05:19,688
A tight umask.

179
00:05:19,688 --> 00:05:22,306
Input checked against an allow list.

180
00:05:22,306 --> 00:05:24,925
The secret loaded from its own file.

181
00:05:24,925 --> 00:05:26,452
And a temporary file,

182
00:05:26,452 --> 00:05:27,762
removed by a trap.

183
00:05:27,762 --> 00:05:29,507
Run it with a good name.

184
00:05:29,507 --> 00:05:31,398
Type: bash secure dot S H,

185
00:05:31,398 --> 00:05:32,344
web zero one.

186
00:05:32,894 --> 00:05:33,547
It works,

187
00:05:33,547 --> 00:05:37,610
and it shows only the last four characters of the token.

188
00:05:37,610 --> 00:05:38,989
Now a hostile name.

189
00:05:38,989 --> 00:05:40,876
Type: bash secure dot S H,

190
00:05:40,876 --> 00:05:43,270
and then in quotes: web zero one,

191
00:05:43,270 --> 00:05:43,996
semicolon,

192
00:05:43,996 --> 00:05:44,286
I D.

193
00:05:44,836 --> 00:05:49,188
Refused. Check the exit code. Type: echo, dollar question mark.

194
00:05:49,738 --> 00:05:49,975
Two.

195
00:05:49,975 --> 00:05:51,933
Safe against the common mistakes,

196
00:05:51,933 --> 00:05:53,594
and honest about the result.

197
00:05:53,594 --> 00:05:55,967
That is a script you can hand to a team.

198
00:05:58,017 --> 00:05:59,340
Four myths to clear up.

199
00:05:59,890 --> 00:06:03,380
Myth one: a secret in a private repository is safe.

200
00:06:03,380 --> 00:06:03,585
No.

201
00:06:03,585 --> 00:06:05,228
Repositories get shared,

202
00:06:05,228 --> 00:06:06,460
cloned and leaked.

203
00:06:06,460 --> 00:06:08,444
Keep secrets out of the code.

204
00:06:08,994 --> 00:06:13,325
Myth two: chmod seven seven seven fixes permission errors.

205
00:06:13,325 --> 00:06:13,549
No.

206
00:06:13,549 --> 00:06:15,864
It gives everyone full control.

207
00:06:15,864 --> 00:06:17,805
Grant only what is needed.

208
00:06:18,355 --> 00:06:20,460
Myth three: quotes are optional.

209
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:20,658
No.

210
00:06:20,658 --> 00:06:21,645
Without quotes,

211
00:06:21,645 --> 00:06:25,592
spaces and special characters split or change your commands.

212
00:06:25,592 --> 00:06:26,974
Quote every variable.

213
00:06:27,524 --> 00:06:30,226
Myth four: blocking bad input is enough.

214
00:06:30,226 --> 00:06:30,429
No.

215
00:06:30,429 --> 00:06:32,253
Allow only what you expect,

216
00:06:32,253 --> 00:06:33,604
and reject the rest.

217
00:06:34,154 --> 00:06:37,332
Secrets. Permissions. Validation. No eval.

218
00:06:39,382 --> 00:06:43,947
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

219
00:06:44,497 --> 00:06:47,633
Keep secrets out of scripts, in a file only you can read.

220
00:06:48,183 --> 00:06:53,751
Set permissions with chmod. Six hundred for secrets, and never seven seven seven.

221
00:06:54,301 --> 00:06:57,715
Use umask zero seven seven for private output.

222
00:06:58,265 --> 00:07:01,913
Validate input with an allow list, and exit with a clear code.

223
00:07:02,463 --> 00:07:05,641
Quote every variable, and never use eval on input.

224
00:07:06,191 --> 00:07:09,797
Never print a whole secret. At most, the last few characters.

225
00:07:10,347 --> 00:07:11,968
To pass, you need all six.

226
00:07:14,018 --> 00:07:14,694
Your turn,

227
00:07:14,694 --> 00:07:17,060
in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

228
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:18,952
Type every command yourself.

229
00:07:18,952 --> 00:07:20,439
Use fake secrets only.

230
00:07:20,989 --> 00:07:26,920
One: create a dot E N V file with a fake token, and lock it with chmod six hundred.

231
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:32,185
Two: write a script that loads it with source, and prints only the last four characters.

232
00:07:32,735 --> 00:07:35,837
Three: create a script without execute permission,

233
00:07:35,837 --> 00:07:36,581
see it fail,

234
00:07:36,581 --> 00:07:38,815
then add execute for the owner only.

235
00:07:39,365 --> 00:07:44,037
Four: set umask zero seven seven, create a file, and read its permissions.

236
00:07:44,587 --> 00:07:47,957
Five: add input validation with a regular expression,

237
00:07:47,957 --> 00:07:51,136
and prove that a name with a semicolon is refused.

238
00:07:51,686 --> 00:07:55,354
Six: write a script that passes a quoted variable to L S,

239
00:07:55,354 --> 00:07:56,384
with two dashes,

240
00:07:56,384 --> 00:07:59,473
and show that a hostile name is not run as code.

241
00:08:00,023 --> 00:08:03,329
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

242
00:08:18,879 --> 00:08:19,754
Quick check.

243
00:08:20,304 --> 00:08:21,754
Where does a password belong?

244
00:08:22,304 --> 00:08:24,950
Which mode locks a secret file to its owner?

245
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:26,886
What does an allow list do?

246
00:08:27,436 --> 00:08:29,143
Why avoid eval on input?

247
00:08:29,693 --> 00:08:35,432
Objective covered: MC05-O07.

248
00:08:35,982 --> 00:08:38,051
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

249
00:08:48,601 --> 00:08:51,481
Next lesson: idempotent scripts and ShellCheck.

250
00:08:52,031 --> 00:08:55,359
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
